{
  "requirement": "HC's issue-content implementation can cache a fetched issue snapshot with a\nfetch time and an application-selected TTL. When caching is enabled, reads\nwithin the TTL reuse that snapshot. After expiry, a network-enabled read obtains\na refreshed snapshot before returning contents, or reports a refresh failure.\nThe returned contents, formatter, and fingerprint remain based on the same\nsnapshot.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The test `src/sdk/issues.rs#mod[tests]/fn[tracked_issue_status_and_fingerprints_follow_refreshed_redacted_snapshots]` covers each obligation in `requirements.sdk.habeas.github_nodes.cache`.\n\n- **Reuse within the TTL:** After the first `verify`, the test calls `node_content` and asserts the fake GitHub request count is still 1. In the loop, `check_inputs(&previous)` runs after the remote response has changed but before `expire`, and it must return true. A refetch would produce a different fingerprint and return false.\n- **Refresh after expiry:** The `expire` helper sets the cache entry's `fetched` to 0. The test then asserts the request count rises to 2 after `check_inputs`. For each of title, body and state, an expired read returns false from `check_inputs`. A new `verify` then returns content containing the new value, and its output matches `state == open`. So the read obtains a refreshed snapshot before it returns.\n- **Refresh failure:** The test expires the cache and makes the fake server return 503. It asserts that `node_content` returns an error mentioning 503.\n- **Contents, formatter and fingerprint from one snapshot:** The test asserts that the first dependency fingerprint equals `supplied.fingerprint()`. It also asserts the parsed title, status and redacted opening post, and that `format()` excludes the token. After each refresh, the new `verify` records a fingerprint that the next `check_inputs` accepts, and `format()` shows the refreshed value. So the contents, formatter and fingerprint stay tied to the same snapshot.\n\nThe TTL is a fixed one-hour constant, `CACHE_LIFETIME` in `src/github.rs`, and the test expires entries by rewriting `fetched`. A separate cache test in `src/github.rs` also exercises the lifetime. The requirement states no behavior for disabled caching or for network-disabled reads after expiry, so I found no gap there.",
  "references": "fc088e639253572ce708a8472168688689a1f8b765d1be38155493246c83ea52",
